Page: 11th January - Hoisting & Closure
Topic: Topic 2: Lexical Environment & Closures > Closures

---

### Definition

A closure is formed when a function retains access to variables from its lexical environment even after the outer function has finished execution.

Closure = Function + Lexical Environment

---

### Basic Closure Example

```javascript
function outer() {
	 let count = 0;

	function increment() {
	    count++;
	    console.log(count);
	}

  return increment;
}

const counter = outer();
counter();
counter();
```

The function increment retains access to count after outer completes.

---

---

### Important Clarification

- Execution contexts are destroyed after execution

- Lexical environments are retained if referenced

- Closures retain lexical environments, not execution contexts

### Some sample interview questions

1. Given a project we need to have less closures or more why ?

1. What’s the output of the snippet ?

```javascript
var rate = 10
function getRate() {
  if (rate == undefined) {
      var rate = 6;
      return rate;
   } else {
      return 10;
   }
}
console.log("Rate is", getRate());
```

1. What’s the output of the snippet ?

```javascript
var x = 1;

function outer() {
  console.log(x);
  var x = 2;

  function inner() {
    console.log(x);
  }

  inner();
}

outer();
```

Page: 10th January - JavaScript Basics
Topic: Topic 2: Execution context > Global Execution Context (GEC) > 2️⃣ Code Execution Phase

- JavaScript executes code line by line

- Variables get actual values

- Functions are invoked

---

#### Example

```javascript
var a = 10; 

function foo() { 
    console.log("Hello");
}

foo();

const test = () = {
  console.log("test")
}

test()
```

Memory Creation Phase:

- a → undefined

- foo → function reference

Execution Phase:

- a → 10

- foo() is invoked

---

### Function Execution Context (FEC)

```javascript
function greetings() {
	console.log("Greetings");
	sayHi();
}

function sayHi() {
	console.log("Hi");
}

greetings();
```

#### What is Function Execution Context?

- Created every time a function is called

- Each function call gets a new execution context

- Pushed onto the call stack

- Destroyed after function finishes execution

---

#### What does FEC contain?

Each Function Execution Context also has two phases:

#### 1️⃣ Memory Creation Phase

- Function arguments are assigned

- Local variables are allocated

- Inner function declarations are hoisted

- this is determined (based on how function is called)

